# core/lib/retired-names.allow
#
# Where a retired distro name may still appear. Read by core/lib/check-repo.sh ("no retired distro names").
#
# Supported targets: Companion OS on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS (resolute) and Companion OS on CachyOS.
# Retired 2026-10-17 (backends removed, restore from the tag pre-retire-multidistro-2026-10-17):
# Linux Mint, Fedora, Bazzite, NixOS (and Ubuntu 24.04; the Ubuntu 24.04 wording is a separate port and is not checked here).
#
# Rules (the guard itself is in check-repo.sh, with the exact patterns):
#   - one path prefix per line: a file, or a directory ending in "/". Blank lines and "#" comments are ignored.
#   - a tracked line that names the restore tag is always allowed (a deliberate "was removed, restore from ..." note).
#     Everything else that names a retired distro or its tooling (dnf, bootc, rpm-ostree, kickstart, lorax, Cinnamon, Nix flakes)
#     must be a path listed here, or it fails the check.
#   - an entry that no longer matches any retired name is STALE and fails the check: delete the line when its file is clean.
#   - "mint" is also an English verb (setup_service.py mints nonces); only the capitalised distro name Mint is matched.
#
# Add only deliberate history. Do not add a file to silence the check on new prose: reword it, or cite the tag.

# --- deliberate history: kept on purpose -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# backends/arch is the provisioning engine under CachyOS, not a target, and frozen (it is never edited). Its comments compare
# it to the Fedora, Mint and NixOS backends it was written next to.
backends/arch/

# The design document explains the retirement and keeps the Arch findings that frozen comments in backends/arch cite by name.
# (Add docs/retired/ here when that directory exists.)
docs/architecture/multi-distro-design.md

# --- a comparison, not history and not a target: needs a maintainer's yes -----------------------------------------------------
# The first-run teardown (docs/architecture/ftue-teardown.md) reads how other major distributions install and start, one of them
# a retired name above, next to Companion OS. It names them on purpose, as the subjects of a measurement made on 2026-10-19; nothing in
# these paths describes a Companion OS target. Rewording would remove the point of the page, so the three paths are listed.
docs/architecture/ftue-teardown.md
docs/evidence/2026-10-19-ftue-teardown/
docs/ui/ftue-teardown/

# --- PENDING: documents the docs phase rewrites. Delete each line when its file is clean (a stale line fails the check) -----

